# A stable name for the modem's AT port, and a way to find out which one it is.
#
# THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY INCOMPLETE ON MONDAY 21 SEPTEMBER 2026, and it
# is incomplete in a way that still does useful work rather than in a way
# that waits.
#
# What is known. 1e0e is SIMCom's USB vendor id. That is not a guess: this
# repository already matches on it for the SIM7600E-H, in
# meta-bench/recipes-bench/bench-router/files/77-sim7600.rules, against a
# part that has been on a board and enumerated.
#
# What is not known. The SIM7070G's product id, and which of its several
# tty interfaces carries AT. Both have to be read off the board with lsusb
# and dmesg, and neither is written here from memory. Project 1 lost two
# rounds to a wlan0 that was assumed, and Project 8 lost a flash to a
# spidev that was.
#
# So the first rule below names every SIMCom tty by its interface number
# instead of naming one of them AT. Plug the HAT in, boot, and:
#
#     ls -l /dev/tracker-tty-*
#     lsusb -d 1e0e:
#     for p in /dev/tracker-tty-*; do
#         printf 'AT\r\n' > "$p"; head -n 2 < "$p"
#     done
#
# The interface that answers OK is the AT port. Record the product id and
# that interface number in projects/16-nbiot-tracker/docs/evidence/, then
# fill in and uncomment the second rule, and the tracker's default port of
# /dev/tracker-at starts working.
#
# Matching on the vendor alone is broader than this repository's habit, and
# it is safe here for a reason worth stating rather than assuming: the
# bench has two SIMCom modems and they are not both plugged in at once,
# because they are both 40-pin HATs and there is one header. If that ever
# stops being true, this rule becomes ambiguous and the product id has to
# be added to it as well.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1e0e", \
	SYMLINK+="tracker-tty-$env{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}"

# The real rule, once the board has been read. Two values to fill in.
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1e0e", ATTRS{idProduct}=="XXXX", \
#	ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="XX", SYMLINK+="tracker-at"

# Nothing on this image manages modems, so there is no ModemManager filter
# policy to argue with and no ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE lines are needed here. That
# is the difference between this image and Project 15's, stated where
# someone comparing the two rule files will look for it.
